out.println("Files in the target directory:");
for (String file : files) {
out.println(file);
}
} catch (Exception e) {
out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
}
%>
```

3. Save the file and upload it to the web application root directory of the Apache Tomcat server.
4. Access the JSP file in your web browser or testing tool by navigating to the URL "/exploit.jsp". If the Ghostcat vulnerability is still present, you will see a list of files in the "/WEB-INF/classes/" directory, which typically contains sensitive information such as source code and configuration files.

V. Mitigation

To mitigate the Ghostcat vulnerability, you should update your Apache Tomcat server to the latest version, as newer versions have fixed this issue. Additionally, you should implement proper input validation and access control checks in your web applications to prevent similar vulnerabilities from arising.
